WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.900
Let's now turn to

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.302
the Pentagon for more on

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.303
what the Coast Guard says

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.171
will be a challenging mission

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.006
to find this

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:07.107
missing submersible

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:09.776
with its five people on board

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.745
again, 13,000 feet deep.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:12.879
Oren, you're at the Pentagon.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:13.680
Tell us more

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.715
about the different search capabilities

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:18.251
that are heading to that area.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:20.053
Alex, a few different types of aircraft

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:21.354
taking part in this search,

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.456
each with their specific search

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.592
and rescue or search and recovery role.

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:26.726
First, both the U.S.

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:28.395
and the Canadians either have launched

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:30.764
or are ready to launch C-130 Hercules.

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:31.398
These are four

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:33.400
engine aircraft designed specifically

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.202
for long, range, long endurance patrols.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:37.170
So they're able to stay up

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.638
for an extended period of time

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.240
and search the surface of the water,

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.975
whether that's for debris

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.343
or of the submersible surface

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.677
and lost communications.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:47.247
So they're likely in charge of that role,

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:49.382
given that this could be quite a large

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:50.583
search area.

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.752
Canada has also launched a P-8 Poseidon.

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:53.420
Brian mentioned

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.988
this is a submarine hunter,

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.057
and that's what its specialty is.

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:58.024
It has different ways

00:00:58.024 --> 00:00:59.692
of detecting the mass

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:01.861
of a body of metal underwater.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.696
And that's what it will try to focus on.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:04.664
The challenge here is that

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:05.732
this submersible

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:08.268
is far smaller than an enemy submarine.

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:09.836
But to its advantage,

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.204
at least this submersible

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:12.405
wants to be found.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.240
One of the ways it will is with

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.642
what are called sonar buoys.

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:17.510
It drops these into the water

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.212
and these listen for any sound

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:20.580
or acoustic signature

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.781
sound obviously travels

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:23.316
very well underwater.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.584
So these can hear from miles

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:25.819
in different directions.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:27.720
And if there is some sound,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.188
you can use

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:29.722
a few of these different sonar buoys

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.092
to try to essentially triangulate or zoom

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:33.526
in precisely

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:34.561
on the location of where

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:35.962
this submersible may be.

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.430
Crucially, for these to work,

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.131
the submersible

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:39.966
has to make some sort of sound.

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.301
So that's one of the key questions

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.302
that remains here.

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:42.902
Earlier today,

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.037
there was a press conference

00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:45.038
from the Coast Guard.

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:46.840
They said
there are commercial ships on the way.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:47.340
They will bring

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.142
additional search capabilities

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:50.643
and they'll keep those aircraft up.

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:51.744
A Canadian Coast

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.646
Guard ship is on its way, having left

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:54.914
Newfoundland a few hours ago,

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.583
according to Marine Traffic AECOM.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.284
It's making its way south, southeast

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.652
towards the search site

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:01.621
towards where that submersible may be.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.223
So it's still hours away.

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:04.124
But they've pointed out

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.125
that there are at least

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:06.593
theoretically, 96 hours

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:07.727
of survival capability

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.963
for the submersible So, Alex,

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.431
the clock is ticking.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:12.832
The search assets are out there

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:14.367
very much looking to see if they can find

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:16.169
where this submersible may be.

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.003
Clock is ticking.

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:17.770
The search is on.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.072
Oren Liebermann at the Pentagon,

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:20.273
thank you very much.

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:22.242
I want to now bring in retired Naval

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.109
Captain Bobby

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:24.711
Scully for more insight

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:26.846
on how the search and rescue effort

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.648
is unfolding.

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:30.116
Captain, thank you so much

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.050
for joining me.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.018
We've been talking

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.087
about the depth of this water,

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:34.921
the distance

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:37.290
that this search site is from the coast.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.459
900 miles off the coast

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:39.926
of Cape

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:41.194
Cod, a little bit less off

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:42.962
off the coast of Canada.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.330
To what extent

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.232
is looking for this submersible

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.567
finally looking at its like

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.068
is it looking like

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.970
a needle in a haystack, if you will,

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:54.374
Well, thanks for having me, Alex.

00:02:54.374 --> 00:02:55.041
And first of all,

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:56.442
I want to say my heart really goes out

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.144
to the crew of the submersible.

00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:00.980
And and also I want to say

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:01.548
good luck

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:02.882
to all the men and women

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.484
that are out there searching.

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:06.386
But as with any

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:09.088
search and rescue operation out at sea,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.991
it is a bit like searching

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.160
for a needle in a haystack.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.262
But in this case,

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:19.432
unlike some of the rescue search missions

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:22.101
that I've been on for aircraft

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.206
in this case, they knew exactly

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:28.808
the location that they were starting with

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:30.310
with the submersible.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.178
So they have put that on

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:34.247
for where to start the rescue,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.716
but where to start the search.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.185
And that's very important in this case.

00:03:39.586 --> 00:03:41.821
So they know a starting point

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:45.525
the depth of water causes this to be

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:49.963
a much bigger locate area to search in.

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:52.932
So that's going to increase the haystack.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.135
But they have

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:59.339
a lot of assets on station already

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:01.307
with the aircraft in the air

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.643
and they're bringing in all the

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:05.645
other resources that they need.

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:08.681
The sonobuoys are fabulous

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:10.683
to get into the water column,

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.819
and that's what they need to do

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:15.421
with having the depth of water that they

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:16.789
they are addressing.

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:19.192
So they have a lot of smart people

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:20.126
in the Coast Guard

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:21.561
and in the Navy

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:23.596
and some of the commercial assets

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.232
to get a multi-pronged approach

00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:28.835
to start chipping away at this haystack.

00:04:29.369 --> 00:04:31.571
And the 96 hours

00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:34.841
is a short amount of time, but.

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:35.441
Right.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.977
These resources are very effective.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:39.078
Captain, you

00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:40.680
you like the Coast Guard, Admiral,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.481
we heard from earlier

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.283
did emphasize the importance

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:45.151
of sonar here.

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:47.820
What is it that those boys

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.656
and those capabilities

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:50.957
will be listening

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:53.259
for under the surface of the water?

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:53.993
How does that work?

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:56.863
Well, with the

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:58.464
sonobuoys they're listening to the sound

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.900
and the submersibles

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.770
going to be making sound itself with the

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:06.005
the systems on board,

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:07.140
the mechanical systems

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.941
and that sort of thing.

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.544
The crew hopefully are making a noise

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:12.412
this as well.

00:05:12.412 --> 00:05:13.646
It can go back to,

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:16.582
you know, the movies that you watch.

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:18.618
You know, when we're hunting submarines,

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:18.985
you know,

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:19.619
the enemy

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:21.187
submarines are not making any noise

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:22.822
because they don't want to be.

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:24.057
They don't want to be found.

00:05:24.057 --> 00:05:24.957
Well, in this case,

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.959
the submersible wants to be found.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:28.861
So a system for running

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.396
the crew is making noise

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:32.699
and those sonobuoys are picking up.

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:33.733
They're very good.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:34.500
And they're picking

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.036
they're detecting these noises.

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:39.172
I imagine that the other systems

00:05:39.172 --> 00:05:40.540
that the ships are bringing

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.743
are the like side scan sonar

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.011
or that type of thing

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.213
that we use typically to pick up

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:49.248
metallic debris on the bottom floor.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:50.083
In this case,

00:05:50.083 --> 00:05:51.818
a metallic submersible,

00:05:51.818 --> 00:05:53.986
which is a larger piece of metal.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:54.854
And so

00:05:55.888 --> 00:05:56.889
imagine that

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:57.290
we're

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.591
bringing in some of the side

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.526
scan sonar that

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.463
or the sonar that picks up metal

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:06.099
in addition to the sonogram

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:09.736
to detect the sound

00:06:09.736 --> 00:06:12.705
So I don't know, but I would imagine

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.374
that between the military

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:15.174
and the Coast Guard

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:17.076
that they're bringing in this other.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:17.744
Right.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.246
It has now been more than 24 hours

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:21.981
since this submarine

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:24.617
and its five passengers went missing.

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:28.154
We understand that there is around

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:31.958
70 to 96 hours of air on board

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:34.894
that that's really the sort of extent

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:36.295
of that.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:36.963
So are you

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:37.964
can you still hold out

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:39.432
hope or are you still optimistic

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:42.902
at this point that this submersible

00:06:42.902 --> 00:06:45.872
can be found with its passengers alive?

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:48.574
Oh, absolutely.

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:49.242
I am,

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:50.643
because the hard part

00:06:50.643 --> 00:06:52.378
is finding the submersible.

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:54.247
And once they find the submersible,

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:56.482
there's all sorts of situations

00:06:56.482 --> 00:06:58.484
of how to get that submersible

00:06:58.584 --> 00:06:59.352
to the surface

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:02.889
and can rescue the crew.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.859
And there's many different options

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:08.194
that just come to my mind

00:07:08.194 --> 00:07:12.198
and I don't have all the details

00:07:12.198 --> 00:07:14.867
of what the situation is.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:15.201
You know,

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:17.170
there is remote operated vehicles

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.738
that can get to that depth

00:07:18.738 --> 00:07:20.807
and they can go down and,

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:21.507
you know, attach

00:07:21.507 --> 00:07:22.875
something to the submersible

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:24.744
if the submersible

00:07:25.378 --> 00:07:26.612
is without power.

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:28.381
And that could be part of the problem

00:07:28.381 --> 00:07:29.482
with communications

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:31.551
and mobility on the commercial.

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:34.053
But we have remote operated vehicles

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:36.155
that can be gotten up to the scene

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:37.924
relatively

00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:38.958
I don't want to say quickly,

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:41.160
but within that time period.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.362
So there's all sorts of scenarios

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:45.731
that very smart people are looking at

00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:48.601
that could recover that.

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:51.437
Well, we're certainly comforted.

00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:53.573
Comforted by your confidence.

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:55.041
Retired Captain

00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:56.809
Bobby Schooley, thank you very much

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:57.210
for your time.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:58.110
And your perspective this evening.

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:00.279
Thank you, Alex.

